  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA broad upper trough is progged to persist across the\nMidwest/Great Lakes/Upper Ohio Valley region through Saturday.\nFollowing this morning's showers/storms, ML CAPE is progged to \nrange from 1000-1750 J/kg this afternoon with 0-6km shear expected\naround 20-30kt. HREF indicates a DCAPE between 600 and 800 \nJ/kg, with a pocket of dry air aloft. Gusty wind will be \npossible in any of the more organized storms. PWATS are also \nexpected to be near 1.8 inches. Heavy downpours continue to be \npossible in some thunderstorms, with a potential for localized \nflash flooding.\n\nThe intensity of the convection should wane this evening as\ndiurnal instability diminishes. An additional vort max within \nthe broad trough is forecast to support showers and isolated \nthunderstorm development after midnight. These showers/storms\nare likely to continue into early Saturday as the shortwave \ncompletes its passage. After a lull in the activity for part of \nthe day, a stronger shortwave within the trough is progged to \ncross the Upper Ohio Valley region late Saturday into Saturday \nnight, with additional showers and thunderstorms, along with the\ncontinued potential for gusty wind and heavy downpours. \n\nGuidance continues to trend toward a dry forecast on Sunday,\nwith brief shortwave ridging behind the stronger Saturday\nshortwave. Dry air and some capping should preclude convection\nthrough the day. \n\nThe broad trough is reestablished across the Great Lakes and\nUpper Ohio Valley region early next week, with daily shower and\nthunderstorm chances returning. Currently, machine learning \nindicates a greater potential for severe weather during this \nperiod, though significant uncertainty remains in the details.",
